--TEST--
Core\Time::parse leaves a field the pattern did not name at the start of its range, where DateTime::createFromFormat() fills it from the clock, and refuses what that member answers false for
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`DateTime::createFromFormat` fills every field its format did not name from **now**, so `createFromFormat("Y-m-d", "2024-02-29")` is that date at the wall-clock time the call happened: the same input parses to a different instant every second, which is why PHP grew `!` and `|` as format characters to turn the behaviour off, and why a program that forgets one has a bug that only shows up in the report it is aggregated into. `Core\Time::parse` reads what the pattern names and leaves the rest at the start of its range — midnight for a date-only pattern — so its answer is a function of its three arguments and nothing else; the first rows below assert exactly that, against the fully written-out spelling of the same instant. That is why this half cannot be an oracle case at all: PHP's answer here is not a constant. Three refusals go with it, and they are two different spec § 10 classes because they are two different mistakes. Text that does not match a well-formed pattern is the *input's* failure — a `ParseError`, where `createFromFormat` answers `false` and files the reason in `DateTime::getLastErrors` for a caller who thought to ask: the mismatch (`"13:45"` against a pattern naming seconds), trailing text nothing consumed, and `"2024-02-30"`, the day February does not have, which PHP normalizes into 2024-03-01 exactly as `mktime` does (`time-at-throws-where-mktime-rolls-a-date-over` owns that rollover). A pattern this *call site* wrote is a bug in the program — a `LogicError`: a letter outside the subset `nvs_stdlib::cldr` carries names itself and says how to quote it, and a **zonal** field is refused however well-formed it is, since `parse` takes a `Zone` as its third argument and a pattern that also named one would give two answers with no rule to pick between them, which is what `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R20 leaves no room for. `createFromFormat` reads `P` from the text and lets it beat the `DateTimeZone` it was passed. The agreeing half — every pattern that names each field it reads — is asserted against PHP itself in `time-parse-matches-create-from-format-over-a-pattern-that-names-every-field`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Read {
    // Each refusal is read as the class it was refused as: the two are one
    // member's answer to two different mistakes, so a row that grew the other
    // one fails here rather than reading as a refusal either way.
    public static function of(string $text, string $pattern): string {
        try {
            return Core\Time::parse($text, $pattern, Core\Time\Zone::UTC)->toInstant()->toEpochSeconds() as string;
        } catch (ParseError $bad) {
            return "ParseError";
        } catch (LogicError $mine) {
            return "LogicError";
        } catch (Throwable $other) {
            return "other";
        }
    }
}

// A pattern that names no time of day at all: every unnamed field is the start
// of its range, so this is the date at midnight and it is that on every run.
echo Read::of("2024-02-29", "yyyy-MM-dd"), "\n";
echo Read::of("2024-02-29 00:00:00", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"), "\n";
echo (Read::of("2024-02-29", "yyyy-MM-dd") == Read::of("2024-02-29 00:00:00", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss")) as string, "\n";

// The same rule one field along: a pattern naming hours and minutes leaves the
// seconds at zero rather than reading a clock for them.
echo Read::of("2024-02-29 13:45", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm"), "\n";
echo (Read::of("2024-02-29 13:45", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm") == Read::of("2024-02-29 13:45:00", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss")) as string, "\n";

// The input's own failures, which `createFromFormat` answers `false` for.
echo "short|", Read::of("2024-02-29 13:45", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"), "\n";
echo "trailing|", Read::of("2024-02-29 13:45:07 and more", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"), "\n";
echo "rolled-over|", Read::of("2024-02-30", "yyyy-MM-dd"), "\n";

// The call site's own, which are the pattern rather than the text.
echo "unknown-letter|", Read::of("2024-02-29", "yyyy-MM-j"), "\n";
echo "offset|", Read::of("2024-02-29 13:45:07+02:00", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ssXXX"), "\n";
echo "zone-id|", Read::of("2024-02-29 13:45:07 Europe/Berlin", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss VV"), "\n";

// The offset PHP would have read from the text is written where this member
// takes it, and the two spellings of that instant agree.
echo Core\Time::parse("2024-02-29 13:45:07", "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss", Core\Time\Zone::of("Europe/Berlin"))->toInstant()->toEpochSeconds(), "\n";
echo Core\Time::fromIso("2024-02-29T13:45:07+01:00")->toEpochSeconds(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
1709164800
1709164800
1
1709214300
1
short|ParseError
trailing|ParseError
rolled-over|ParseError
unknown-letter|LogicError
offset|LogicError
zone-id|LogicError
1709210707
1709210707
